<h2>The prosecution story</h2>
<p>The allegation was that in May 2015, the appellant sexually assaulted an 11-year-old girl with a 40% intellectual disability at her home. The child stayed overnight with her cousin, who is said to have told the victim's mother the next day. The matter surfaced only when the child spoke to her schoolteacher in June, and the FIR followed on 8 June 2015 — ten days after the alleged incident. The trial court convicted under Section 6 POCSO (aggravated penetrative sexual assault); the High Court affirmed. On the surface, concurrent findings of fact — the hardest terrain for any appeal.</p>

<h2>Where it unravelled</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Dock identification failed</strong><p>Neither the victim nor her cousin could identify the appellant in court; both said the mother had supplied the name. The victim admitted the prosecutor told her what to say.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>FIR omissions</strong><p>The last-seen circumstance relied on at trial was missing from the FIR; the mother had seen the appellant only plucking leaves in the garden, not with the child.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Withheld witness</strong><p>The schoolteacher — the first person outside the family the child spoke to — was never examined, drawing an adverse inference against the prosecution.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Alibi and medical evidence</strong><p>The defence alibi was credibly proved yet brushed aside below, and the medical witnesses' testimony could not be relied on because the defence had no effective chance to cross-examine them.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The legal architecture of the acquittal</h2>
<p>Two doctrinal points anchor the judgment. First, on omissions: an FIR "is not expected to be an encyclopaedia," but where a circumstance later relied upon is a material incriminating fact, its absence from the earliest version is significant. The last-seen theory, central to the conviction, appeared nowhere in the FIR. Second, on Section 106 of the Evidence Act: the burden of explaining facts especially within a person's knowledge shifts only after the prosecution establishes the foundational circumstance — here, that the accused and the victim were last seen together. That was never shown, so the burden never moved. With identity unproved, delay unexplained, a material witness withheld and an alibi established, the cumulative effect made the conviction unsafe. The Court set aside both judgments and directed the appellant's release.</p>

<h2>What this means for POCSO trials and appeals</h2>
<p>POCSO prosecutions rightly receive legislative and judicial solicitude, including the presumptions in Sections 29 and 30. This judgment is a reminder that none of that displaces the first question of every criminal trial: is this the man? Where the victim is very young or intellectually disabled, investigators must build identification through means that do not depend on courtroom recognition years later — prompt test identification, consistent first statements, and examination of the earliest confidants. For the defence, the case maps the classic pressure points in an appeal against concurrent findings: unexplained FIR delay, material omissions measured against the trial narrative, withheld witnesses, untested medical evidence and a properly proved alibi. Concurrent findings protect a sound conviction; they cannot immunise an unsound one.</p>

